Good question — thanks for asking. I added both the server-side checkboxes and the DT colvis button because they solve different problems and give different user experiences. Here’s the reasoning, tradeoffs, and what I recommend.

Why both were included (intent and benefits)

    Server-side checkboxes (explicit, persistent)
        Persistent state: selections live in Shiny reactive state (input$cols). That makes the visible-column choice available to the server and other UI logic, and it persists across paging, sorting, and re-render cycles.
        Control & actions: you can programmatically change the selection (select-all / select-none / select-preferred), validate “no columns selected” cases, or persist the user’s choice across sessions if you want.
        Bandwidth/control for big data: when you intentionally choose a smaller set of columns server-side, you can avoid sending unnecessary columns to the client (important if you later adopt server-side processing or large tables on Gadi).
        Integration: it integrates naturally with our preferred-column ordering logic (we compute order server-side then intersect with selected columns).
    DT colvis (client-side convenience)
        Instant, no round-trip: toggling via the DataTables Columns button is immediate and purely client-side, which is nice for quick exploration.
        Familiar UI: many users expect the DataTables “Columns” menu for ad-hoc show/hide.
        Lightweight: does not require additional Shiny inputs or re-rendering to quickly hide a column.

Tradeoffs / why having both can be redundant/confusing

    Potential for mismatch: The two controls can get out of sync — a user toggles in the DT’s colvis but the server-side checkbox input still shows columns as selected (or vice-versa). That can confuse users when selections don’t match.
    Redundancy: If you always want server-authoritative show/hide, the DT colvis is redundant. If you prefer client-side only exploration, the checkbox group is redundant.
    Complexity: Maintaining parity (e.g., syncing client-side toggles back to Shiny) requires extra JS or proxy code.

Recommendations (pick one, or sync)

    If you want server-authoritative control (recommended for:
        large datasets,
        actions that depend on which columns are visible,
        wanting selections to persist across pages/sorts), then keep server-side checkboxes and remove the DT colvis button. That gives a single source of truth and predictable behavior.
    If you want fast, lightweight, exploratory toggling and the app will always send the full table to the client (small tables), then remove the checkboxes and keep colvis only.
    If you want both convenience and server-awareness, implement synchronization between DT’s column visibility and the Shiny checkboxes:
        Use DT + DataTables API to capture colvis changes and push them to Shiny (via JS -> Shiny.setInputValue), and/or use dataTableProxy + JS to apply checkbox changes to the visible columns without full re-render.
        That requires a small JS bridge but gives best of both worlds (client immediacy + server state).

Concrete implementation options I can do for you

    Option A (make authoritative server-side): remove the DT Buttons/colvis option and keep only checkboxGroupInput; the table is always rendered server-side with only selected columns.
    Option B (client-side only): remove the checkbox UI and enable only DT colvis; lighten server workload.
    Option C (sync both): add a tiny JS snippet so DataTables colvis updates input$cols, and update DT visibility immediately when input$cols changes using dataTableProxy. This requires:
        adding DT extension Buttons (we already have that),
        adding JS callback to listen for column-visibility.dt events and call Shiny.setInputValue(ns("cols_from_dt"), visibleColsArray, {priority: "event"}),
        an observer that updates the checkboxGroupInput when cols_from_dt changes (and vice-versa update colvis via proxy when checkboxes change).

Which approach do you want? I can implement any of the three. If you want my recommendation: go with Option A (server-side authoritative) for reliability on a cluster like Gadi and if you may work with larger tables; pick Option C only if you want both immediacy and persistent server state and are willing to add the small JS glue.

