Nice — good design question. I’ll walk through options, tradeoffs, and a recommended, practical server-authoritative design that fits the app you already have (server-side visibility/ordering, filtering by name, running on Gadi).

High-level goals to satisfy

    User can reorder columns interactively in the Data tab.
    The app respects an initial “preferred order” coming from Home.
    Reordering in the Data UI changes the displayed order immediately (and can optionally become the new preferred order).
    Filtering always operates by column name (not by position), so reordering never breaks filters.
    Server remains authoritative (so visibility, ordering and filtering are consistent, persists across pages/sorts, and works for large tables).

UI options for reordering (pros / cons)

    Drag-and-drop list of column names (server-side):
        Pros: very clear UX, easy to make server-authoritative (reordered list posted to Shiny), accessible to persist/save, small data traffic (only names move).
        Cons: requires a small JS widget or an R wrapper (e.g., sortable/rank_list, shinyjqui/orderInput).
    DataTables ColReorder extension (client-side header drag):
        Pros: intuitive — users drag headers directly.
        Cons: client-side by default; to make server authoritative you must capture JS events and send new order to Shiny (extra JS glue). Also risk of out-of-sync state if you don't sync both ways.
    Up/Down buttons or arrow controls per column:
        Pros: simple to implement with no JS; accessible/keyboard-friendly.
        Cons: slower for large numbers of columns, clunkier UX.
    Selectize-like ordered input (selectize with ordering):
        Pros: compact UI; users can search and reorder.
        Cons: less visually obvious than drag handles and may need custom code to persist order.

Recommendation (best balance of UX, reliability, and simplicity)

    Use a drag-and-drop server-side list of column names in the Data tab (e.g., rank_list from the sortable package or orderInput from shinyjqui). Treat that as the canonical display order in the app.
    Keep server-side checkboxes for visibility (as you already chose). The workflow becomes:
        Home load: parse preferred order string and store it (shared_data$preferred_cols).
        When data loads, compute the initial display order = (preferred_cols ∩ file_cols) in user order, then append any remaining file_cols.
        Show checkboxGroupInput for visibility and a drag-and-drop ordered list UI for ordering. Initialize both to the initial display order (checkboxes check the same columns).
        When the user reorders via drag-and-drop, Shiny receives the new order (input$col_order_from_ui); update a server-side display_order reactive (shared_data$display_order) immediately and re-render the DT using that order and the currently selected visible columns.
        Filtering uses column names (e.g., df$VALUE) so filtering is unaffected by order.
        Provide a “Save as preferred” control (or auto-save toggle) that, when used, stores shared_data$preferred_cols <- shared_data$display_order so next time the file loads the saved order is used.
        Add “Reset to preferred” and “Reset to original file order” actions so users can recover if they mess up the order.

How behavior would work in your example

    Original TSV columns: [ID, VALUE]
    Home preferred string: "VALUE;ID" -> on load shared_data$preferred_cols = c("VALUE","ID")
    On load initial display_order computed as c("VALUE","ID") and checkboxes select both columns.
    User then reorders in the app to [ID, VALUE] using the drag list. That action updates shared_data$display_order to c("ID","VALUE") immediately and the Data tab re-renders in that order.
    User performs filtering (slider on VALUE). Filtering code should refer to df$VALUE (i.e., by column name), so it finds the VALUE column regardless of its current index. The filtered result is computed correctly; the Data tab then re-renders the filtered rows in the display_order the user last set.
    If they press “Save as preferred”, shared_data$preferred_cols is updated to c("ID","VALUE") and becomes the new default for subsequent loads. If they don’t press Save, the change is session-local only.

Why this approach meets constraints

    Server-authoritative: visibility and order are controlled server-side and stored in reactiveValues, avoiding out-of-sync issues and making the state available to other server logic (e.g., exporting only visible columns).
    Robust filtering: filters operate by name (not index), so reordering is purely presentation and never breaks filter semantics.
    Performance-friendly: for large tables you can subset columns server-side before sending to the client (only send visible columns in user order), reducing bandwidth.
    Good UX: drag-and-drop is intuitive; checkboxes provide explicit control and persistence.

Implementation notes you'd want later (if/when coding)

    Preferred libraries for the drag UI: sortable (rank_list) or shinyjqui::orderInput are both solid. They both produce an input value in Shiny that is an ordered character vector.
    Always reconcile orders by intersecting with current colnames() to handle cases when a different file is loaded (e.g., pref mentions missing columns).
    Keep two server-side vectors:
        shared_data$preferred_cols — persisted initial preference from Home (or last saved).
        shared_data$display_order — current UI order (used for rendering). This separation allows “save” semantics and easy resets.
    Keep filtering functions keyed by column names, and validate that required filter columns exist before applying filters; show a clear warning if not.
    Provide small UX helpers: “select preferred”, “reset to file order”, and a small confirmation when Save-as-preferred occurs.
    Accessibility: ensure drag handles have keyboard alternatives (e.g., up/down arrows) if you want full keyboard support.

Edge cases and how to handle them

    Preferred list contains names not in the file: ignore missing names and inform the user (don’t error).
    User reorders before data loads: ignore or disable the drag UI until data is present.
    Column name collisions or identical names: normalize/validate column names on load (or warn the user).
    Very wide tables: consider adding search/filter for column list, or collapse into groups to keep the reorder UI usable.

Summary / final recommendation

    Implement a server-side drag-and-drop reorder control (rank_list/orderInput) + server-side checkboxes for visibility. Use display_order (server reactive) as the source of truth for rendering; keep preferred_cols as the saved default that can be updated by an explicit “Save as preferred” action. Always filter by column name. This gives the best mix of UX, reliability, and maintainability (especially on a cluster like Gadi where server authority is desirable).

